IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `theft-nouns`

-- Theft Act 1968, sections 26 to 29: search for stolen goods, evidence and
-- procedure, and summary trial. Layer 1.
--
-- These sections are mostly powers of justices, constables and courts. They are
-- encoded only for what they mean for the person who may be accused: whether their
-- premises can be searched, whether they can be tried with others, what evidence
-- can be used to show they knew or believed goods were stolen, whether a statutory
-- declaration can be used against them, and whether the offence can be tried
-- summarily with their consent. What a justice or court does inside those limits
-- is not encoded.
--
-- Section 28 and sections 26(2), 26(4), 27(4A) and 29(1) are shown as dots in this
-- text and say nothing.

§ `Sections 26 to 29 - enforcement and procedure`

§§ `Section 26 - search for stolen goods`

-- src:200 | (1) If it is made to appear by information on oath before a justice of the peace that there is reasonable cause to believe that any person has in his custody or possession or on his premises any stolen goods, the justice may grant a warrant to search for and seize the same; but no warrant to search for stolen goods shall be addressed to a person other than a constable except under the authority of an enactment expressly so providing.
-- src:201 | (2) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:202 | (3) Where under this section a person is authorised to search premises for stolen goods, he may enter and search the premises accordingly, and may seize any goods he believes to be stolen goods.
-- src:203 | (4) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:204 | (5) This section is to be construed in accordance with section 24 of this Act; and in subsection (2) above the references to handling stolen goods shall include any corresponding offence committed before the commencement of this act.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 26 (src:200-204)
GIVEN j IS A `Search facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26(1) a justice may grant a warrant to search for and seize stolen goods` j MEANS
        j's `information on oath has been laid before a justice of the peace`
    AND j's `it has been made to appear that there is reasonable cause to believe that a person has stolen goods in his custody or possession or on his premises`
    AND `s 26(1) the warrant may be addressed to the person named` j

-- s 26(1), last words. A warrant to search for stolen goods is addressed to a
-- constable, or to someone else only under an enactment expressly so providing.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 26(1) (src:200)
GIVEN j IS A `Search facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26(1) the warrant may be addressed to the person named` j MEANS
        j's `the warrant is addressed to a constable`
    OR  (    j's `the warrant is addressed to someone other than a constable`
         AND j's `an enactment expressly provides for a warrant to be addressed to someone other than a constable`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 26(3) (src:202)
GIVEN j IS A `Search facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26(3) he may enter and search the premises` j MEANS
    j's `he is authorised under section 26 to search the premises for stolen goods`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 26(3) (src:202)
GIVEN j IS A `Search facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26(3) he may seize any goods he believes to be stolen goods` j MEANS
        j's `he is authorised under section 26 to search the premises for stolen goods`
    AND j's `the searcher believes the goods to be stolen goods`

§§ `Section 27 - evidence and procedure on a charge of theft or handling stolen goods`

-- src:206 | (1) Any number of persons may be charged in one indictment, with reference to the same theft, with having at different times or at the same time handled all or any of the stolen goods, and the persons so charged may be tried together.
-- src:207 | (2) On the trial of two or more persons indicted for jointly handling any stolen goods the jury may find any of the accused guilty if the jury are satisfied that he handled all or any of the stolen goods, whether or not he did so jointly with the other accused or any of them.
-- src:208 | (3) Where a person is being proceeded against for handling stolen goods (but not for any offence other than handling stolen goods), then at any stage of the proceedings, if evidence has been given of his having or arranging to have in his possession the goods the subject of the charge, or of his undertaking or assisting in, or arranging to undertake or assist in, their retention, removal, disposal or realisation, the following evidence shall be admissible for the purpose of proving that he knew or believed the goods to be stolen goods:—
-- src:209 | (a) evidence that he has had in his possesion, or has undertaken or assisted in the retention, removal, disposal or realisation of, stolen goods from any theft taking place not earlier than twelve months before the offence charged; and
-- src:210 | (b) (provided that seven days’ notice in writing has been given to him of the intention to prove the conviction) evidence that he has within the five years preceding the date of the offence charged been convicted of theft or of handling stolen goods.
-- src:211 | (4) In any proceedings for the theft of anything in the course of transmission (whether by post or otherwise), or for handling stolen goods from such a theft, a statutory declaration made by any person that he despatched or received or failed to receive any goods or postal packet, or that any goods or postal packet when despatched or received by him were in a particular state or condition, shall be admissible as evidence of the facts stated in the declaration, subject to the following conditions:—
-- src:212 | (a) a statutory declaration shall only be admissible where and to the extent to which oral evidence to the like effect would have been admissible in the proceedings; and
-- src:213 | (b) a statutory declaration shall only be admissible if at least seven days before the hearing or trial a copy of it has been given to the person charged, and he has not, at least three days before the hearing or trial or within such further time as the court may in special circumstances allow, given the prosecutor written notice requiring the attendance at the hearing or trial of the person making the declaration.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27 (src:206-213)
GIVEN j IS A `Joint handling facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(1) he may be charged in one indictment with others and tried with them` j MEANS
        j's `he is charged with handling all or any of the stolen goods from a theft`
    AND j's `others are charged in the same indictment with handling goods from the same theft, at different times or at the same time`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27(2) (src:207)
GIVEN j IS A `Joint handling facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(2) the jury may find him guilty whether or not he handled the goods jointly` j MEANS
        j's `two or more persons are indicted for jointly handling stolen goods`
    AND j's `the jury are satisfied that he handled all or any of the stolen goods`

-- s 27(3)(a). A theft "not earlier than twelve months before the offence charged":
-- only the earlier end is read, as the text states no later end (NOTES.md, fork 23).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27(3)(a) (src:209)
GIVEN e IS A `Previous dealings evidence facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(3)(a) evidence of other recent dealings in stolen goods is admissible` e MEANS
        e's `he is being proceeded against for handling stolen goods and for no other offence`
    AND e's `evidence has been given of his having, or arranging to have, the goods in his possession, or of his undertaking or assisting in, or arranging to undertake or assist in, their retention, removal, disposal or realisation`
    AND e's `the evidence is that he has had in his possession, or undertaken or assisted in the retention, removal, disposal or realisation of, stolen goods from another theft`
    AND `add months` (e's `the date of that other theft`) 12 AT LEAST (e's `the date of the offence charged`)

-- s 27(3)(b). A conviction "within the five years preceding the date of the offence
-- charged" is one on a day before that date and not earlier than five years before
-- it; "seven days' notice in writing" is read as at least seven days.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27(3)(b) (src:210)
GIVEN e IS A `Previous dealings evidence facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(3)(b) evidence of a conviction in the last five years is admissible` e MEANS
        e's `he is being proceeded against for handling stolen goods and for no other offence`
    AND e's `evidence has been given of his having, or arranging to have, the goods in his possession, or of his undertaking or assisting in, or arranging to undertake or assist in, their retention, removal, disposal or realisation`
    AND e's `the evidence is that he has been convicted of theft or of handling stolen goods`
    AND (e's `the date of that conviction`) LESS THAN (e's `the date of the offence charged`)
    AND `add years` (e's `the date of that conviction`) 5 AT LEAST (e's `the date of the offence charged`)
    AND e's `days of written notice given to him of the intention to prove the conviction` AT LEAST 7

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27(3) (src:208-210)
GIVEN e IS A `Previous dealings evidence facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(3) evidence is admissible to prove he knew or believed the goods to be stolen goods` e MEANS
        `s 27(3)(a) evidence of other recent dealings in stolen goods is admissible` e
    OR  `s 27(3)(b) evidence of a conviction in the last five years is admissible` e

-- s 27(4). The person charged may defeat the declaration by giving the prosecutor
-- written notice, at least three days before the hearing or trial (or within such
-- further time as the court allows), requiring the declarant to attend.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 27(4) (src:211-213)
GIVEN d IS A `Statutory declaration facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(4) the statutory declaration is admissible as evidence of the facts stated` d MEANS
        d's `the proceedings are for the theft of anything in the course of transmission, or for handling stolen goods from such a theft`
    AND d's `the declaration says that the declarant despatched or received or failed to receive goods or a postal packet, or that they were in a particular state or condition when despatched or received`
    AND d's `oral evidence to the like effect would have been admissible in the proceedings`
    AND d's `days before the hearing or trial that a copy of the declaration was given to the person charged` AT LEAST 7
    AND NOT (    d's `the person charged gave the prosecutor written notice requiring the declarant to attend`
             AND (    d's `days before the hearing or trial that he gave that notice` AT LEAST 3
                  OR  d's `the court allowed further time in special circumstances and he gave the notice within it`))

§§ `Section 29 - summary trial`

-- src:219 | (2) In Schedule I to the Magistrates’ Courts Act 1952 (which lists the indictable offences by adults which may be tried summarily with the consent of the accused) for paragraph 11 there shall be substituted:—
-- src:220 | 11
-- src:221 | Any indictable offence under the Theft Act 1968 except—
-- src:222 | (a) robbery, aggravated burglary, blackmail and assault with intent to rob; and
-- src:223 | (b) burglary comprising the commission of, or an intention to commit, an offence which is not included in this Schedule; and
-- src:224 | (c) burglary in a dwelling if entry to the dwelling or the part of it in which the burglary was committed, or to any building or part of a building containing the dwelling, was obtained by force or deception or by the use of any tool, key or appliance, or if any person in the dwelling was subjected to violence or the threat of violence; and
-- src:225 | (d) handling stolen goods from an offence not committed in the United Kingdom.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 29(2) (src:219-225)
GIVEN t IS A `Summary trial facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 29(2) paragraph 11 lists the offence as one that may be tried summarily with his consent` t MEANS
        t's `the offence is an indictable offence under the Theft Act 1968`
    AND NOT (t's `it is robbery, aggravated burglary, blackmail or assault with intent to rob`)
    AND NOT (t's `it is a burglary comprising the commission of, or an intention to commit, an offence which is not included in that Schedule`)
    AND NOT (    t's `it is a burglary in a dwelling`
             AND (    t's `entry to the dwelling, or to the part of it where the burglary was committed, or to any building or part of a building containing the dwelling, was obtained by force or deception or by the use of any tool, key or appliance`
                  OR  t's `a person in the dwelling was subjected to violence or the threat of violence`))
    AND NOT (t's `it is handling stolen goods from an offence not committed in the United Kingdom`)
